WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.267
-You look amazing!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:04.801
-Jimmy, I cannot with you.

00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:05.934
You always give me
the best love.

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:07.934
It's the Virgo energy
for me every time.

00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:10.300
-I give you that --
I wanted to say hi to the baby.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.868
Hi, baby!
I cannot wait to meet you.

00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:14.634
-The baby said, "What's up?
Can't wait to be besties."

00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:16.167
-I love it, I love it.

00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:18.033
I said, "Is there anything
I can get you?"

00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:19.667
-You got me hooked up
with this back support.

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.434
-I got a pillow there.
You wanted a LaCroix.

00:00:22.434 --> 00:00:23.601
-Thank you for
this LaCroix, honey.

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:25.701
-I got you a LaCroix.
I got whatever you want.

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:27.334
-Thank you so much.
Keeps the stomach settled.

00:00:27.334 --> 00:00:28.467
You're really
looking out for me.

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:30.901
-I have to.
I'm so excited about this.

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:32.067
And, by the way -- You know me.
I love you so much.

00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:33.400
-I love you, too.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.801
-But when you announced it on
"Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:37.834
that was the best thing.

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:40.000
[ Cheers and applause ]
You said, "Ta-da!"

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.133
-Yeah. Every time
I look back at that clip,

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:45.434
I'm like, "Why did I sound
like a villain?"

00:00:45.434 --> 00:00:46.901
"I am..."

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:50.267
I don't know why I said it like
this, but I was so excited.

00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:52.200
I mean, I always thought
to myself, you know,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.567
I would love the opportunity,
obviously, to host "SNL."

00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:56.834
I just did not know
it would be the same time

00:00:56.834 --> 00:00:58.167
that I was pregnant.

00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:00.200
And so I was like,
"I guess I got to say it."

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.634
-Yeah.
-I got to come out with it.

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:02.934
-You can't time that stuff out.

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:05.000
It's just everything magical
happened for a reason.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.033
And it was the greatest --
I have a picture of you.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.434
-Oh, my gosh! Yeah!
-The reveal.

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:10.133
It was just the best thing
I've ever seen.

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:12.701
It was so good.
Everyone went nuts.

00:01:12.701 --> 00:01:16.067
Everyone went --
It was just great.

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:18.567
And, by the way,
you hosted a great episode.

00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:20.767
You were unbelievable.
-Thank you so much.

00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:22.267
-They loved you up there.

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:23.367
Everyone was like,
"Oh, we love Keke."

00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:24.868
-I had so much fun.

00:01:24.868 --> 00:01:27.434
I mean, I just felt like I was
really getting some real skills

00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:29.767
going down,
I mean, from Steve to Lorne.

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:30.968
They were really just
giving it to me.

00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:32.234
And all the cast.

00:01:32.234 --> 00:01:34.100
I just felt really like
I was a part of a team.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.534
-Yeah.
-And I love that.

00:01:35.534 --> 00:01:37.300
I mean, from theater, with doing
Broadway and stuff like that,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.400
I love to be a part of
a cast like that,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.434
so it was awesome.

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:41.234
-Even from, like,
the first promo,

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:42.634
the commercial I saw,
I go, "Oh, no.

00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:44.033
Keke's gonna destroy this one."

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:45.801
-You knew I was ready.
-Oh, yeah.

00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:48.634
You got Kenan and Kel...
-Oh, my gosh! Yes!

00:01:48.634 --> 00:01:50.300
-...to reunite.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.667
[ Cheers and applause ]
-So...

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:56.901
-Talk to me about this.
-Shout-out to Brian Robbins.

00:01:56.901 --> 00:01:59.534
Obviously, shout-out to
Kenan Thompson, Kel Mitchell.

00:01:59.534 --> 00:02:02.834
I grew up on "Kenan and Kel."
I've always loved that show.

00:02:02.834 --> 00:02:05.801
And just the com-- I mean, I'm a
huge fan of multicam sitcom,

00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:07.601
which we don't see
a ton of anymore, right?

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:09.601
-I miss it, I miss it.
-I miss it so much.

00:02:09.601 --> 00:02:11.501
And just the satire
and the drama,

00:02:11.501 --> 00:02:13.300
but also the heart of it all.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.701
And it's so funny,
because, you know,

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:16.767
nowadays, whenever they
bring something back

00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:19.767
or they -- you know, it becomes
this deep-seated drama.

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:22.834
And so I loved that when I came
to Kenan with the idea,

00:02:22.834 --> 00:02:24.367
I was like, "Man, we should do
something like 'Kenan and Kel,'

00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:25.801
but like 'Kenan and Kelly.'"

00:02:25.801 --> 00:02:27.567
You know what I mean?
So I could be Kelly.

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:28.667
-Smart.

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:31.367
-And he immediately,
like, got on it,

00:02:31.367 --> 00:02:33.234
you know,
wrote this great sketch

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:35.868
with his writing partner
over at "SNL."

00:02:35.868 --> 00:02:37.801
And they came up with
this really funny sketch,

00:02:37.801 --> 00:02:39.067
which is what you guys saw.

00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:42.934
-Was Kel always in it?
-So, I didn't mention Kel.

00:02:42.934 --> 00:02:44.601
That was a surprise to me.

00:02:44.601 --> 00:02:46.467
When he -- When I actually
read the sketch,

00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:48.400
I'm like, "You got Kel."

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.501
-Oh, my gosh.

00:02:49.501 --> 00:02:51.100
-I'm like, this --
'Cause Kel is it!

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.400
You know what I'm saying?

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.400
Like, You think about Kel --
I mean, first of all,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.701
Kel is another Virgo, like us.

00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:57.367
From Chicago.
We're from the same city.

00:02:57.367 --> 00:03:01.667
So I was so excited to get the
opportunity to work with Kel.

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:04.501
And I was actually in
the real throwback story.

00:03:04.501 --> 00:03:06.367
Like, it was
a dream come true for me.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:07.801
-I mean, you crushed it.
-Thank you.

00:03:07.801 --> 00:03:10.634
-Now I'm thinking --
Speaking of Virgos, baby --

00:03:10.634 --> 00:03:12.701
do we know what --
I know you're into signs and --

00:03:12.701 --> 00:03:17.167
-Exactly. So, my baby is either
going to be a Pisces or Aries.

00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:18.767
I'm not sure exactly.

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:20.734
Like, I mean, Pisces --
Let me know, guys.

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:22.901
I mean, Pisces are known
to be very deep.

00:03:22.901 --> 00:03:24.400
They're emotional creatures.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.834
So I just want to make sure I'm
not too blunt for my baby boy.

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:28.133
I don't want to be too --
-No.

00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:30.334
-You know, I don't want to be
like, "Look, what's up?"

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:31.667
You know, I want to
make sure that I'm --

00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:33.167
You know, because sometimes,
I can be a little too

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:35.200
"tell it like it is," you know?

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.701
I need to wade in the water with
a Pisces, wade in the water.

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:42.667
Just keep it chill, child.
-Keep it chill for now.

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:45.567
I'm just amazed
at everything you're doing.

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.400
I want to talk to you about
a couple things.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.601
But this is
some recent highlights

00:03:48.601 --> 00:03:49.934
that happened this year
with Keke Palmer.

00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:52.300
-What ya gonna tell me?
-Well, I'll tell you some stuff.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.000
Your movie "Nope"
was named one of

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.834
AFI's Top 10 Movies of the Year,
by the way.

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:57.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:03:57.934 --> 00:04:00.067
-You crushed it!

00:04:00.067 --> 00:04:01.200
Couldn't take your eyes
off of you.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.601
-Oh, my gosh, stop.

00:04:02.601 --> 00:04:04.300
-You were named
Best Supporting Actress

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.868
by the New York Film Critics
Circle for "Nope."

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:08.701
Come on.

00:04:08.701 --> 00:04:11.033
"New York Times" named you their

00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:13.734
list of best actors of the year,
Keke Palmer!

00:04:13.734 --> 00:04:17.701
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:04:17.701 --> 00:04:23.133
And you were nominated for
three NAACP Image Awards.

00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:24.634
That's what I'm talking about.
-Oh, my gosh. Thank you.

00:04:24.634 --> 00:04:25.801
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:04:25.801 --> 00:04:27.801
Keke Palmer!

00:04:27.801 --> 00:04:30.234
-Jimmy, I cannot with you.
You're the best.

00:04:30.234 --> 00:04:31.467
Thank you.

00:04:31.467 --> 00:04:33.634
-I just kind of freak out
at all the cool things.

00:04:33.634 --> 00:04:34.734
One of the awards
is for hosting "Password."

00:04:34.734 --> 00:04:35.901
Come on, buddy!

00:04:35.901 --> 00:04:37.200
-I was going to say,
and "Password," okay?!

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.300
-Come on, buddy! Come on, bud!
-Exactly. That's us.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.467
-That's my favorite.
It's so much. I love it.

00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:44.367
Let's talk about this podcast.
-Oh, yes.

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:45.767
"Baby, this is Keke Palmer."

00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:47.634
-"Baby, this is Keke Palmer."

00:04:47.634 --> 00:04:50.801
What do people expect
when they listen to this?

00:04:50.801 --> 00:04:53.033
-So, they can expect --
I mean, I think one of

00:04:53.033 --> 00:04:54.634
the biggest things about me --
anybody that knows me --

00:04:54.634 --> 00:04:56.334
is I'm extremely curious.

00:04:56.334 --> 00:04:57.834
I love to ask questions.

00:04:57.834 --> 00:04:59.701
And so you're going get
a bunch of different topics,

00:04:59.701 --> 00:05:03.801
from dating to, you know,
how we, you know,

00:05:03.801 --> 00:05:05.167
live in this world
with social media.

00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:06.300
How do we grow?

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.534
How do we live with
being pregnant?

00:05:07.534 --> 00:05:08.868
Like, just all of
these different experiences

00:05:08.868 --> 00:05:10.167
that we're going to be having.

00:05:10.167 --> 00:05:11.968
But the thing that I love
the most about getting to do

00:05:11.968 --> 00:05:15.067
a podcast is that I also get to
have long conversations

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:16.834
and, most importantly,
with experts,

00:05:16.834 --> 00:05:18.734
with some people that actually
specifically are in

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:19.901
some of these fields.

00:05:19.901 --> 00:05:21.234
I'm always trying to get
into their heads.

00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:22.367
You know what I'm saying?
-Yes, of course.

00:05:22.367 --> 00:05:24.567
-Like, let me get
Neil deGrasse Tyson on.

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:26.300
What is really out there, Neil?

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.000
-Yes. Talk to us.
-Let me talk to Tom.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Did you realize what you were
doing when you created Myspace?

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.367
I want to know the truth.
-Tom from Myspace.

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:38.000
-Let me call up
the pastor and say,

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.868
"What's really going on
in the church?"

00:05:39.868 --> 00:05:42.300
-Ooh!

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.033
But you do -- you get in there.
-Yeah.

00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:46.133
-And people will tell you.
-Yeah, they do. They do.

00:05:46.133 --> 00:05:48.834
I get so many --
I mean, I just had a --

00:05:48.834 --> 00:05:51.100
this really great
dating expert on there,

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.634
who I was talking to, and I had
this big question like,

00:05:52.634 --> 00:05:53.701
"What do you do when someone
comes to you and they're like,

00:05:53.701 --> 00:05:54.868
'I'm really picky'?"

00:05:54.868 --> 00:05:56.133
You know you guys hear that
all the time.

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:57.534
"Oh, I'm really picky.

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:58.934
You know, it's always just --
You know, it's hard for me

00:05:58.934 --> 00:06:00.067
'cause I'm really picky."

00:06:00.067 --> 00:06:01.534
And she said,
one of the biggest things

00:06:01.534 --> 00:06:03.601
when someone tells her that
they're really picky,

00:06:03.601 --> 00:06:05.234
the first thing she thinks is,

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:06.534
"You really don't know
what you want."

00:06:06.534 --> 00:06:07.734
That's, like, the biggest sign.

00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:09.234
When someone says they're picky,
a lot of times,

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:11.367
it's because they really
actually don't know how to --

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:13.667
They don't know how to narrow
down what it is they really want

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:15.501
and what matters to them,
so they just say,

00:06:15.501 --> 00:06:16.734
"I'm really picky."

00:06:16.734 --> 00:06:18.467
And then she said,
on top of that,

00:06:18.467 --> 00:06:21.200
it makes her think that,
"Are you really actually willing

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.567
to do the work that it takes
to open yourself up to someone?"

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:27.501
And, so, you just kind of coax
it with, "I'm picky."

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:29.133
But, really, it's because
you don't really want to do

00:06:29.133 --> 00:06:30.234
what it takes.

00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:31.701
And, so, I was like --
I mean, I just --

00:06:31.701 --> 00:06:33.501
It's something casual,
but when you really

00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:35.334
get in deep down into it --
I mean, I have a homegirl

00:06:35.334 --> 00:06:36.868
that's always saying she picky,
and deep down,

00:06:36.868 --> 00:06:38.300
I know she ain't looking
for no man.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.434
-Yeah.

00:06:39.434 --> 00:06:41.133
-She ready to go to work.

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:42.501
-Yeah, you got to put
the work in.

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:44.067
You got to put the work in.

00:06:44.067 --> 00:06:46.834
And then talk to me about
this digital TV network.

00:06:46.834 --> 00:06:48.267
-Oh, yeah. So, KeyTV.

00:06:48.267 --> 00:06:49.901
So, yeah, you know,
I've been in the industry

00:06:49.901 --> 00:06:53.267
at this point -- it's so crazy
to believe -- 20 years, man.

00:06:53.267 --> 00:06:54.334
-Is that right?

00:06:54.334 --> 00:06:55.968
-20 years,
your girl been working!

00:06:55.968 --> 00:06:57.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:57.801 --> 00:07:01.400
So, 20 years
I've been in the industry.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.434
And I think, just for me,
I kept thinking to myself,

00:07:03.434 --> 00:07:06.868
"What can I do
to get outside of just me?"

00:07:06.868 --> 00:07:09.000
I think, obviously,
as you know as an entertainer,

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.767
everything is always about you
and what the next gig

00:07:11.767 --> 00:07:13.067
I'm going to do
and how I'm going to do this

00:07:13.067 --> 00:07:14.100
and how I'm going to do that.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.400
And I think
I just thought to myself,

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.434
"Okay, with the skills
that I've acquired thus far

00:07:17.434 --> 00:07:19.834
and the different experiences
I've had as an actor,

00:07:19.834 --> 00:07:21.701
as a producer,
you know, hopefully growing

00:07:21.701 --> 00:07:24.801
into a writer and director,
how can I transcend that

00:07:24.801 --> 00:07:27.400
into something outside of myself
for, you know,

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.467
my generation or my peers
and other people

00:07:29.467 --> 00:07:31.200
that want to do
what I want to do?"

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.033
And so that's where
KeyTV came from.

00:07:33.033 --> 00:07:34.634
It's the keys to
the culture, okay?

00:07:34.634 --> 00:07:36.434
We're speaking to
the generation,

00:07:36.434 --> 00:07:39.367
the Gen Z, Millennials
that want to do --

00:07:39.367 --> 00:07:40.834
that want to be in entertainment
but don't know exactly

00:07:40.834 --> 00:07:42.000
how to get there.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.734
So it's about entertainment,
great content,

00:07:44.734 --> 00:07:47.300
hopefully, if you check it out,
but then also

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.133
education and
the democratization of how to

00:07:49.133 --> 00:07:51.200
actually get from
point "A" to point "B."

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:53.634
-Yeah, let's make some stars.

00:07:53.634 --> 00:07:55.334
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Let's make some stars.

00:07:55.334 --> 00:07:56.834
You know what I mean?
Let's do it.

00:07:56.834 --> 00:07:58.734
-I love that.
I love everything you do.

00:07:58.734 --> 00:08:00.934
Keke and I are playing
a game when we come back,

00:08:00.934 --> 00:08:02.167
"The Jinx Challenge."
-I can't wait for this.

00:08:02.167 --> 00:08:04.467
-Come on back and get ready
for this. Come on.

